.\" Man page for nic-sweep.sh
.\" Written in classic troff/groff format
.TH NIC-SWEEP 8 "2026-10-27" "nic-sweep 2.16.0" "System Administration Commands"
.SH NAME
nic-sweep \- collect nic-health (and nic-xray) from every host of an inventory over SSH
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nic-sweep.sh
.BR \-f | \-\-inventory
.I FILE
.RI [ \-s | \-\-site
.IR SITE ]...
.RI [ \-J | \-\-jump
.IR HOST ]
.RI [ \-u | \-\-user
.IR USER ]
.RI [ \-p | \-\-parallel
.IR N ]
.RI [ \-T | \-\-timeout
.IR SEC ]
.RI [ \-o | \-\-output\-dir
.IR DIR ]
.RI [ \-l | \-\-level
.IR basic | deep ]
.RI [ \-I | \-\-interval
.IR SEC ]
.RI [ \-L | \-\-lookback
.IR MIN ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-xray ]
.RB [ \-\-ship
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-accept\-new\-hostkeys ]
.RI [ \-n | \-\-no\-color ]
.RI [ \-v | \-\-version ]
.RI [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nic-sweep.sh
is the fleet companion of
.BR nic-health.sh (8)
and
.BR nic-xray.sh (8).
It reads an inventory, connects to each host over SSH (in parallel), runs
.B nic-health \-O json
and, where installed,
.BR "nic-xray \-\-all \-\-output json" ,
and writes one sweep directory per site:
.PP
.RS
.nf
DIR/<site>/<YYYYmmddTHHMMSSZ>/
  sweep.json      how the sweep ran: version, options, inventory hash,
                  start/end, host counts by status, verdict and nic-xray
  MANIFEST.tsv    host, address, status, exit_code, duration_s, xray, verdict
  merged.csv      every nic-health result, prefixed with site, hostname,
                  collected_at, version, level, interval_s, uptime_s, boot_id
  nodes/<host>.json       nic-health JSON, byte for byte
  nodes/<host>.xray.json  nic-xray JSON (when nic-xray is installed)
  nodes/<host>.err        stderr of both tools
.fi
.RE
.PP
The per-host JSON is the record of the sweep; every other file can be rebuilt
from it.
.B merged.csv
always carries how the data was collected (tool version, level, window, time,
uptime and boot ID), which a plain
.B nic-health \-O csv
does not.
.PP
The tool is read-only on the hosts: it never installs or changes anything.
It never prompts: SSH runs with
.B BatchMode=yes
and the tools with
.BR "sudo \-n" ;
a host that would ask for a password is recorded as a failure instead.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-inventory " " \fIFILE\fR
Hosts to sweep (required), one per line:
.IR site , host , address [, user ].
.I host
names the host in the sweep;
.I address
is what SSH connects to (name or IP).
Blank lines, lines starting with
.B #
and a
.B site,host,address,user
header are ignored. Every field must match
.B [A-Za-z0-9._-]+
(the address may also contain
.BR : );
a line that does not, or a duplicate host within a site, is reported and skipped.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-site " " \fISITE\fR
Only sweep this site. Repeatable. Default: every site in the inventory.
.TP
.BR \-J ", " \-\-jump " " \fIHOST\fR
SSH jump host,
.RI [ user @] host [: port ],
passed to
.BR "ssh \-J" .
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user " " \fIUSER\fR
SSH user for inventory lines without one. Default: the user from your SSH
configuration.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-parallel " " \fIN\fR
Hosts swept at once (1\-256, default 8).
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-timeout " " \fISEC\fR
Wall-clock limit for nic-health on one host (1\-7200). Default: the interval plus
300 seconds in deep mode, plus 120 seconds in basic mode. nic-xray has its own
120-second limit.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output\-dir " " \fIDIR\fR
Root of the sweep tree (default
.BR ./sweeps ).
Directories are created with mode 0750 and files with 0640. An existing sweep
directory is never overwritten.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-level " " \fIbasic\fR|\fIdeep\fR
nic-health level (default
.BR deep ).
.TP
.BR \-I ", " \-\-interval " " \fISEC\fR
nic-health sampling window (0\-3600, default 30). A window of 30 seconds or more
catches bursty link errors that a 5-second window misses.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-lookback " " \fIMIN\fR
nic-health link-down look-back: driver link-down messages in the kernel journal
over the last MIN minutes, current boot only (0\-1440, 0 = off). Without this
option nic-sweep does not pass
.B \-L
and nic-health's own default (60) applies. Use 1440 to cover the last 24 hours.
Recorded as
.B options.lookback_min
in
.B sweep.json
.RB ( null
when not given).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-xray
Do not collect nic-xray. By default it is collected on every host where
.B nic-xray
(or
.BR nic-xray.sh )
is on the remote PATH; hosts without it are recorded as
.B absent
in the manifest, not as a failure.
.TP
.BI \-\-ship " PATH"
Send this local
.B nic-health.sh
to each host over the SSH connection, write it to a private temporary file under
.BR /tmp ,
run it with
.B sudo \-n
and delete it on exit (also on HUP, INT or TERM). For hosts without the package.
The SSH user needs sudo rights for
.BR "bash /tmp/nic-health.*" ,
which amounts to unrestricted root; prefer the package where that is not
acceptable. Without this option the
remote
.B nic-health
(or
.BR nic-health.sh )
on the PATH is used.
.TP
.B \-\-accept\-new\-hostkeys
Trust unknown host keys on first contact
.RB ( StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new ).
By default your SSH host key policy applies.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-color
Disable color and the progress bar (both are off when output is not a terminal).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the help and exit.
.SH HOST STATUS
.TP
.B ok
nic-health returned its JSON (any verdict).
.TP
.B ssh-fail
SSH could not connect or authenticate (exit 255).
.TP
.B sudo-fail
.B sudo \-n
refused (a password or terminal would be needed, the command is not allowed),
or sudo is not installed.
.TP
.B tool-missing
Neither
.B nic-health
nor
.B nic-health.sh
is on the remote PATH.
.TP
.B timeout
The host did not finish within
.BR \-\-timeout .
.TP
.B bad-output
Anything else: the output is not nic-health JSON with a known verdict (strict JSON:
non-finite numbers such as
.B \-inf
are rejected even though
.BR jq (1)
accepts them), or the collection ended without a result.
.PP
nic-xray is recorded separately as
.BR ok ,
.B absent
(not installed),
.B failed
(error or invalid JSON) or
.B skipped
(\fB\-\-no\-xray\fR, or the host was unreachable or timed out).
.SH NOTES
The remote commands are run by the SSH user's login shell, which must be a POSIX
shell (sh, dash, bash, ksh, zsh); a csh or fish login shell yields
.BR bad-output .
Skipped inventory lines are reported when the sweep starts and counted in
.BR sweep.json ;
they do not change the exit status.
The remote commands run with
.BR LC_ALL=C ,
so sudo messages can be classified. When the host has
.BR timeout (1),
the remote tool is limited to the local timeout minus 5 seconds, so a dropped
SSH connection leaves no long-running root process. Interrupting
.B nic-sweep.sh
stops every running collection before it exits.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.IP \(bu 2
Bash 4.4 or newer on the operator host, run as an unprivileged user.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR ssh (1),
.BR timeout (1),
.BR jq (1),
.BR sha256sum (1),
.BR getopt (1).
.IP \(bu 2
On each host:
.B nic-health
(package) or
.BR \-\-ship ,
passwordless
.B sudo
for the SSH user to run it, and optionally
.BR nic-xray .
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /usr/bin/nic-sweep
.TQ
.B /usr/bin/nic-report
The fleet commands, as packaged.
.TP
.B /usr/share/nic-xray/nic-report-rules.json
The packaged diagnosis rule catalog read by
.BR nic-report.py (8).
.PP
The
.B nic-xray-fleet
package, meant for the operator or jump host, ships
.B nic-sweep
and
.B nic-report
with their man pages and bash completions, and the rule catalog. It requires
.B nic-xray
of the same version,
.BR jq (1),
an SSH client and Python 3.9 or newer
(python311 on openSUSE Leap 15.6), and suggests WeasyPrint
(python3-weasyprint) for PDF reports.
Fleet nodes need only the
.B nic-xray
package, which carries
.BR nic-health .
.SH EXIT STATUS
Like nic-health, plus collection failures. A CRIT is never hidden behind an
unreachable host.
.TP
.B 0
Every host collected, all verdicts OK.
.TP
.B 1
Every host collected, at least one WARN and no CRIT.
.TP
.B 2
At least one CRIT, even if some hosts could not be collected.
.TP
.B 3
No CRIT, but at least one host was not collected; or no WARN and at least one
host reported UNKNOWN; or a usage error.
.PP
Precedence: CRIT > a host not collected > WARN > UNKNOWN > OK. Among verdicts this
is the order nic-health uses.
.SH EXAMPLES
Inventory (one file can hold several sites):
.PP
.RS
.nf
site,host,address,user
SITE1,node-ctrl-01,10.0.0.15
SITE1,node-cmp-01,10.0.0.20,ops
SITE2,node-ctrl-01,192.0.2.15
.fi
.RE
.PP
Sweep every site, deep mode, 30-second window:
.PP
.RS
.B nic-sweep.sh \-f inventory.csv
.RE
.PP
One site, through a jump host, 16 hosts at a time:
.PP
.RS
.B nic-sweep.sh \-f inventory.csv \-s SITE1 \-J admin@jump.example \-p 16
.RE
.PP
Flap evidence for the last 24 hours:
.PP
.RS
.B nic-sweep.sh \-f inventory.csv \-L 1440
.RE
.PP
Hosts without the package: ship the local script:
.PP
.RS
.B nic-sweep.sh \-f inventory.csv \-\-ship ./nic-health.sh
.RE
.PP
Build an inventory from Juju machines of one model (site name supplied):
.PP
.RS
.nf
juju machines \-m openstack \-\-format json |
  jq \-r '.machines[] | ["SITE1", .hostname, .["dns-name"]] | @csv' | tr \-d '"'
.fi
.RE
